From 480dadbf500fc1e454109d42a23468517ddb136a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:07:29 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Stream COPY and TYPE instead of reading whole files into
 memory (#16)

COPY read the whole host file into memory before writing it, and TYPE
read the whole VMS file into memory before printing it, so memory use
grew with the file: a 192 MiB file took about 226 MiB peak either way.

The library gains ods2_content_source_t and ods2_create_file_source():
the content's length is known up front, so allocation is decided from
it exactly as before, then write_extents() pulls 1 MiB chunks from the
source and writes them extent by extent. If the source fails part way
(a read error, a file that shrank) the existing undo path gives back
every block and file number and no file is created.
ods2_create_file() and ods2_create_file_attrs() are now thin wrappers
over a memory source.

ods2_read_file_stream() hands a file's content to a sink a chunk at a
time, telling a sink failure apart from a volume read error;
ods2_read_file() wraps it with a memory sink.

In the CLI, COPY reads a regular file directly, sized by fstat(); input
with no length (a pipe, /dev/stdin) is spooled to a tmpfile() first.
TYPE writes each chunk to stdout as it is read. Measured with the same
192 MiB file: COPY 226 -> 13 MiB peak, TYPE 225 -> 13 MiB.

Tests: ods2_large_file_selftest checks a streamed source failing after
some chunks leaves no file and leaks nothing, with no chunk over
ODS2_STREAM_CHUNK; cli_large_file_check.sh checks a 96 MiB file stays
under 64 MiB peak through COPY (file and pipe) and TYPE, byte for byte.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WA1cTxSh5f8UrT87d9WhcQ
